  "Question": "For what value of \\(x\\) does \\[10^{x} \\cdot 100^{2x}=1000^{5}?\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The bases \\(100\\) and \\(1000\\) are powers of \\(10\\), so using a common base lets us compare the exponents. For real \\(x\\), the positive-base power laws give\n\\[\n10^x\\cdot100^{2x}\n=10^x\\cdot(10^2)^{2x}\n=10^{x+4x}=10^{5x}.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(1000^5=(10^3)^5=10^{15}\\). Thus the equation becomes \\(10^{5x}=10^{15}\\).\n\nSince powers of \\(10\\) increase strictly as their real exponents increase, equal powers must have equal exponents. Therefore \\(5x=15\\), so \\(\\boxed{x=3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
